      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_B821705D_CONCEPTS_01",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR__B821705D_1",
          "title": "1. Key Academic Principles: Gravitational Potential Energy and Gravitational Potential",
          "contentMarkdown": "Gravitational potential energy of two point masses m1 and m2 separated by distance r is U(r) = - G * (m1 * m2) / r. Gravitational potential V at distance r from mass M is the potential energy per unit mass: V(r) = - G * M / r. Gravitational potential energy U(r) is always negative for finite separation with zero reference at infinite separation.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_B821705D_F2BA44A7",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_B821705D_E74E7270",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_B821705D_30720FDC"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_B821705D_CONCEPTS_02",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR__B821705D_2",
          "title": "2. Key Academic Principles: Gravitational Potential Energy and Gravitational Potential",
          "contentMarkdown": "The relationship between gravitational field intensity E_g and gravitational potential V is E_g = - dV/dr. Near Earth's surface, the change in gravitational potential energy for height h is Delta U = m * g * h. Gravitational potential is a scalar quantity with SI unit joules per kilogram (J/kg).",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_B821705D_C8D8F6A8",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_B821705D_0A0E33A4",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_B821705D_A969A111"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_B821705D_CONCEPTS_03",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR__B821705D_3",
          "title": "3. Key Academic Principles: Gravitational Potential Energy and Gravitational Potential",
          "contentMarkdown": "Since gravitational force is attractive, U(r) is always negative for finite separations. V(r) = U(r) / m = - G * M / r. Gravitational potential V(r) is defined as potential energy per unit mass: V(r) = U(r) / m = - G * M / r.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_B821705D_83583E84",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_B821705D_2DF33491",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_B821705D_BCF6EEFC"
          ]
        }
      ],
